            "sub": "the array language family"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "family",
      "seconds": 30,
      "heading": "Five languages",
      "narration": "{APL|@apl} showed that a language could treat arrays and math as its basic vocabulary. {J|Jay} explored an {ASCII|ass key} notation, and powerful function composition. {K|Kay} kept a compact vocabulary, and short expressions. After them, {BQN|@bqn} revisited many of these ideas, with a modern notation and combinators. {X_eTaL|@xtl} takes ideas from this family, but makes different choices.",
      "lines": [],
      "slide": {
        "type": "points",
        "steps": [
          0,
          1,
          2,
          3,
          4
        ],
        "items": [
          {
            "text": "APL",
            "sub": "arrays and math as the vocabulary"
          },
          {
            "text": "J",
            "sub": "ASCII notation, function composition"
          },
          {
            "text": "K",
            "sub": "a compact vocabulary"
          },
          {
            "text": "BQN",
            "sub": "a modern notation, combinators"
          },
          {
            "text": "X_eTaL",
            "sub": "checked types, ASCII rendered, composition"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "not-replacement",
      "seconds": 12,
      "heading": "A family, not a replacement",
      "narration": "{X_eTaL|@xtl} is not a replacement for any one of them. It borrows from the family. Then it makes its own choices.",
      "lines": [],
      "slide": {
        "type": "points",
        "steps": [
          0,
          1,
          1
        ],
        "items": [
          {
            "text": "Not a replacement",
            "sub": "for any one of them"
          },
          {
            "text": "Borrowed ideas",
            "sub": "its own choices"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
